readers.why:
Nearly every line predicts something you could see on the screen:
- a timed footnote four minutes after the last message;
- two-word bursts followed by a topic change ten seconds later;
- a soft emoji in place of the final period;
- an uncapitalised sentence with typing bubbles that vanish;
- the tell "which is standard for me, really."

The "late night" and "on something" lines blur together, since both are manic analytical sprints. Parts of "waiting" and "on something" describe his body (the bathroom mirror, hands under his thighs) rather than his typing.
